Spring AOP(Aspect Oriented Programming)

- AOP is a feature of the spring framework that helps you separate cross-cutting concerns (like logging, security, transactions) 
  from your main business logic.


What problems does Spring AOP solve?
- In large applications, some functionality (Logging, Security Checks, Transactional Managements, Caching etc) is needed across multiple modules,  
- Without AOP, this code gets repeated everywhere, making the system messy and hard to maintain.
- Spring AOP lets you write this code once and apply it everywhere needed.
- Spring AOP works by allowing you to define aspects that can "intercept" method execution in your application.

Key Terms:
1. Aspect:
 A module that contains cross-cutting logic.
 Ex: Logging Aspect

 @Aspect
 @Component
 public class LoggingAspect { }

2. Join Point
 A point in the execution of a program. (usually a method call)

3. Advice
 The action taken at join point. Types: 
 a) before: Runs before a method
 b) After: runs after method.
 c) Around: wraps method execution
 d) AfterReturning: After method successfully returns
 e) AfterThrowing: If method throws exception

 Example:
	@Before("execution(* com.example.service.*.*(..))")
	public void logBefore() {
    		System.out.println("Method started");
	}

4. Point Cut
 - Define where the advice should be applied(which methods)
 - or an expression that selects join points.
Example: execution(* com.example.service.UserService.*(..))

 
5. Weaving:
 Process of linking aspects with other objects (done at runtime in spring)

How Spring AOP works??
Spring AOP uses proxies:
 - It creates a proxy object around your target object or bean.
 - Client calls the proxy
 - Proxy executes the advice.
 - Proxy invokes target method.
 - Proxy executes post advice
Business logic remains untouched


Implementation
1. Dependency
in pom.xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-aop</artifactId>
</dependency>

2. Add Aspect file
@Aspect
@Component
public class LoggingAspect {

    @Before("execution(* com.example.service.*.*(..))")
    public void logBeforeExecution() {
        System.out.println("Method execution started");
    }
}

3. Target Class
@Service
public class OrderService {
    public void placeOrder() {
        System.out.println("Order placed");
    }
}

Output
Method execution started
Order placed


@Around Advice Example (Most Powerful)
@Around("execution(* com.example.service.*.*(..))")
public Object logExecutionTime(ProceedingJoinPoint joinPoint) throws Throwable {
    long start = System.currentTimeMillis();
    Object result = joinPoint.proceed();
    long end = System.currentTimeMillis();
    System.out.println("Time taken: " + (end - start));
    return result;
}




Extra notes:
1. All aspects are placed in a dedicated package for better organization.
e.g. src/main/java/com/example/project/
 - controller/
 - services/
 - repository/
 - model/
 - aspects/
 - config/

So, aspect is typically inside com.example.project.aspect
src/main/java/com/example/project/aspect/LoggingAspect.java

2. Spring will only detect your aspect if its inside a component-scanned package.

3. @Aspect should be also used with @Component because spring only processes beans it manages.

